---
// Help: claiming authorship (docs/ACCOUNTS.md, "Author verification"; docs/CONTRIBUTIONS.md,
// "Claim" and "Moderation"; the automatic moderator's rules: oscr/moderation.py, /policies/moderation/).
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { REVIEW_DAYS } from "../../lib/moderation";

const toc = [
  { id: "automatic", text: "When your ORCID iD is on the paper" },
  { id: "claim", text: "Otherwise, a claim" },
  { id: "decided", text: "How a claim is decided" },
  { id: "maintainer", text: "Maintainers of the code" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Claiming authorship"
  description={`How an author of a paper becomes its verified author on ${SITE_NAME}: automatically with ORCID, or by a claim that published rules verify.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/claim/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    Being a paper's <strong>verified author</strong> on {SITE_NAME} lets you correct its links, validate its tracing
    map, and have your removal requests applied at once. Most authors become one without asking anything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="automatic">When your ORCID iD is on the paper</h2>
  <p>
    Sign in with ORCID (or link your ORCID identity to your account). Every paper whose published metadata lists your
    ORCID iD among its authors is then yours at once, and appears under "Your papers" on your{" "}
    <a href="/account/">account page</a>. The check runs at each sign-in; "Check my papers again" runs it on request,
    after {SITE_NAME} has read a new paper of yours, for instance. It relies on the iDs that publishers deposit. An iD
    counts only when it is in the paper's own metadata; the site does not guess an iD from a name.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claim">Otherwise, a claim</h2>
  <p>
    Many papers list the iDs of only some of their authors. If you are an author and the paper's metadata does not have
    your iD, open the paper's page, signed in, and use "Claim this paper" in its Contribute section: say why you are one of its authors
    (1,000 characters at most, no email address) and, if you can, give a page that shows it (your lab's page, your ORCID
    record listing the paper). Your account page shows the claim and its answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="decided">How a claim is decided</h2>
  <p>
    By published rules (<PageLink href="/policies/moderation/">the moderation policy</PageLink>), which {SITE_NAME}'s
    machine applies within minutes. A claim is <strong>verified</strong> when the paper lists your ORCID iD among its
    authors, or when Crossref's automatic update has put the paper in your ORCID record, which it does once the paper's
    publisher deposited your iD with it. A waiting claim is checked again every day. You then become a verified author of
    the paper, a role the automatic check never takes back. Link your ORCID identity to your account: the rules look for
    the paper in that iD's record.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The rules do not count a work you added to your ORCID record yourself, or through a search tool, because anyone
      can add any paper to their record. They also do not count a statement or a web page; only the operator reads
      those.</li>
    <li>In the other cases the claim waits for the operator, who may accept it. At the moment no human moderator is on
      duty, so a claim not verified within {REVIEW_DAYS} days is closed, with how to claim again. A new claim is checked
      again. A decision of the operator stays in force.</li>
    <li>The operator may also take back a role the rules granted by mistake.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>At most 20 claims may wait at once for an account, and 10 author claims may be sent a day.</p>

  <h2 id="maintainer">Maintainers of the code</h2>
  <p>
    Maintaining a paper's code is a different role. It is proven on the forge that hosts the code, such as GitHub: see{" "}
    <a href="/help/accounts/#roles">roles</a>. A maintainer who owns the repository, or is a public member of its
    organization, may correct what the record says of it. A contributor cannot.
  </p>
</Info>
